---
import Project from "@layouts/Project.astro";

export const frontmatter = {
    title: "Rainbow School",
    coverImage: "/deathandrevival/Landscape 2.jpg",
};
---

<Project
    title={frontmatter.title}
    subtitle="A Journey Through Light, Instinct, and Play"
    description="Rooted in the belief that learning emerges through play, this kindergarten invites children to engage with space through curiosity, challenge, and subconscious exploration. The design began with two conceptual seeds: a blindfolded drawing exercise and the folklore of Red Riding Hood. The resulting drawing revealed a woven rope-like section, inspiring a massing strategy of overlapping cubes that form spatial volumes. The landscape echoes this logic, composed of square platforms and connective slits that encourage movement and discovery."
    coverImage={frontmatter.coverImage}
    coverImageAlt={frontmatter.title}
    course="Architectural Studio II"
    courseDescription="Learning by Playing or A Kindergartener's Approach to Architecture"
    instructor="Daniel Horowitz"
    collaborators=""
    date="Spring 2020"
    location=""
>
    <section class="main">
        <p>
            Rooted in the belief that learning emerges through play, this
            kindergarten invites children to engage with space through
            curiosity, challenge, and subconscious exploration. The design began
            with two conceptual seeds: a blindfolded drawing exercise and the
            folklore of Red Riding Hood. The resulting drawing revealed a woven
            rope-like section, inspiring a massing strategy of overlapping cubes
            that form spatial volumes. The landscape echoes this logic, composed
            of square platforms and connective slits that encourage movement and
            discovery.
        </p>
        <p>
            Drawing from lesser-known versions of Red Riding Hood—where the
            protagonist undergoes trials before reaching her end—the school
            becomes a metaphorical journey. Children navigate the building
            through climbing, crawling, and walking, fostering both passive and
            active knowledge through embodied interaction. The architecture
            challenges and supports, offering a playground of layered cognition
            where instinct precedes logic. flourish once more.
        </p>
        <p>
            Throughout the day, colored glazing filters sunlight into shifting
            patterns of light and shadow. Polygonal windows cast vibrant shapes
            across floors and walls, creating an ever-changing environment that
            quietly reinforces lessons in color, form, and perception. The name
            Rainbow School reflects this choreography of light—a spatial
            language that speaks to children before they learn to name it.
        </p>
    </section>

